Abstract 
&nbsp;
This study examined the influence of political patronage on voters&rsquo; behavior and party loyalty in Mati, San Miguel, Zamboanga del Sur. Guided by Moustakas&rsquo; phenomenological framework, it explored the lived experiences of electors through in-depth interviews, focusing on how socio-economic conditions, cultural obligations, and generational perspectives shape electoral decision-making. The findings revealed that political patronage remains deeply embedded in community life, functioning both as a survival mechanism and a cultural practice. Older generations largely no

